    "blocks": [
      {"id": "title", "type": "markdown", "body": "# Winning Strategy: Month-by-Month Attribution"},
      {
        "id": "executive_summary",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "month_attribution",
        "body": "## Executive Summary\n\nThe frozen winning strategy was **long-driven and short-hedged**. Across January 2024–August 2025, it produced **9.94% annualized net excess return**, **5.77% realized volatility**, a **1.72 Sharpe ratio**, a **2.64 HAC t-statistic**, and a **−1.96% maximum drawdown**. The result was positive in **13 of 20 months**.\n\nThe long sleeve contributed **+14.13% annualized** before costs; the short sleeve contributed **−3.19% annualized**; modeled costs consumed **0.99% annualized**. That negative average short P&L does not mean the shorts failed as a hedge: they were profitable in **all six negative-market months**, and their monthly contribution had a **−0.82 correlation with the market factor**. The short book sacrificed return during many rallies but materially stabilized weak-market months."
      },
      {"id": "headline_metrics", "type": "metric-strip", "cardIds": ["sharpe", "net_excess", "cumulative_return", "risk", "significance"]},
      {
        "id": "monthly_section",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Monthly Evidence\n\nThe sleeve chart separates what the long and short books contributed before portfolio-level costs. A profitable short contribution means the securities held short fell relative to cash; a negative short contribution means they rose and hurt the portfolio."
      },
      {"id": "sleeves", "type": "chart", "chartId": "sleeve_contributions", "layout": "full"},
      {
        "id": "regime_section",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "month_attribution",
        "body": "## The Short Book Earned Its Place Through Protection\n\nIn positive-market months, the portfolio averaged **+0.96% net excess per month**: longs contributed **+2.19%**, while shorts cost **−1.15%**. In negative-market months, the portfolio still averaged **+0.51% net excess**: longs lost **−1.20%**, but shorts earned **+1.79%**. This is the clearest economic explanation for the high Sharpe ratio."
      },
      {"id": "regimes", "type": "chart", "chartId": "regime_attribution", "layout": "full"},
      {
        "id": "path_section",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "month_attribution",
        "body": "## Return Path and Stress Months\n\nThe strongest month was **July 2024 (+4.98% net excess)**, when both sleeves made money. **February 2025 (+2.25%)** was the purest hedge success: longs lost 1.58%, while shorts earned 3.91%. The worst month was **December 2024 (−2.07%)**, but shorts still added 2.13% and prevented a much larger loss from the long book."
      },
      {"id": "path", "type": "chart", "chartId": "cumulative_path", "layout": "full"},
      {
        "id": "ledger_section",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Month-by-Month Diagnosis\n\nThe ledger below is the complete 20-month path. It shows the market backdrop, net excess return, sleeve attribution, exposures, the largest security-level contributor and detractor, and the economic interpretation of each month."
      },
      {"id": "ledger", "type": "table", "tableId": "monthly_detail", "layout": "full"},
      {
        "id": "mechanics",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "month_attribution",
        "body": "## What the Optimizer Was Actually Doing\n\nThe strategy used roughly **0.97× average gross exposure** and **0.28× average net long exposure**. Net exposure was usually 0.30×, but fell toward 0.20× in several months. Forecast volatility ranged from **3.42% to 5.42%**, well below the 10% ceiling, so the volatility cap was not the main binding control. Turnover reached the **0.50 monthly cap in every month**, making turnover and transaction costs operationally important even though monthly modeled cost stayed within roughly 7–10 basis points."
      },
      {
        "id": "conclusion",
        "type": "markdown",
        "sourceId": "month_attribution",
        "body": "## Decision Interpretation\n\nThe winning strategy should be described as a **high-quality long selector paired with an expensive but effective defensive short sleeve**. Its success did not require the shorts to make money on average; it required them to make money when the long book and the market were weak. The month-by-month record supports that mechanism.\n\nThe fixed short-floor specification beat the optional-short counterfactual in **11 of 20 months**, but the difference was unstable and previously failed statistical superiority tests. Because changing the short constraint also changes the long allocation, those monthly differences are a joint portfolio effect—not isolated proof that the floor itself creates alpha."
      },
      {
        "id": "caveats",
        "type": "markdown",
        "body": "## Caveats and Next Evidence\n\nThis is a short, 20-month historical development window, not untouched production confirmation. The strategy was selected using evidence available through August 2025, so September 2026 onward remains the clean confirmation boundary. Security-level contribution labels use CRSP date-effective tickers, and their displayed contributions are pre-cost stock-excess contributions; portfolio costs are shown separately. The result is economically and statistically meaningful on its own path, but superiority over nearby optimizer constraints has not been established."
      }
    ]
  },
